S5-F1 verified. **CONFIRMED**, severity lowered to Medium.

What I proved:
- `_engine_env` (`runtimes.py:508-531`) builds one `prefixes` list over all `manifest.items()` with no engine argument, then one PATH — so every engine's lab bin goes to every spawn.
- On this Mac, `lab-paths.json` holds only `{"optiq": …/optiq-0.5.13/bin}`, and `shutil.which("python", path=_engine_env()["PATH"])` returns **OptiQ's venv python**. OptiQ's venv is a real venv (`python -> python3.14`) with `mlx_lm 0.31.3` on **mlx 0.32.4**; the control venv has `mlx_lm 0.31.3` on **mlx 0.32.2**. Both print `0.31.3` from `python -m mlx_lm --version`, so `parse_version` cannot distinguish them.
- The runner scripts' `export PATH="…/mlx-lm-0.31.3/bin:$PATH"` does **not** save it — `_engine_env` prepends ahead of ambient, so OptiQ wins even with the export in place.
- No source or row records the mlx core version anywhere.

Two corrections to the finding, both narrowing it: `mlxlm` is the **only** start command that is a generic name (the other five are unique); and **no mlx-lm cell has been measured since the lab copy appeared** — `lab-paths.json` was born 2026-10-30 13:24, the only mlx-lm canary is 2026-10-29 16:15. So no published row is contaminated yet, which is why I lowered High to Medium; the defect goes live on the next control cell.

